course: arts
lesson: 4
title: Starting a band
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A band is a team where everyone has a job: the drums keep the beat, the bass plays the low notes, a guitar or keyboard plays chords, and someone sings; the bass and drums together are the rhythm section, the floor the whole song stands on.
explain: Picture three friends arguing for an hour about calling themselves "Skibidi Wolves" or "The Toast" without playing a note: the name is the least important part, and lots of famous bands changed theirs early on.
explain: Most bands begin with covers, songs written by other people, with cheap gear or even pots and buckets, and with a short plan for each practice; skills come from playing, not from shopping.
explain: Bands break up over bossiness, no-shows and volume wars, so good bands turn up, share decisions and play at a level where everyone can be heard, and protect their ears from loud drums.
explain: Bands talk in their own slang: in Liverpool "our kid" is your brother or sister, "la" is mate and "boss" is great; in Australia a "galah" is a fool (from a noisy pink parrot) and "yeah nah" means no; in the southern US "fixin' to" means about to.
fact: Many famous bands changed their names early on, and it is smart to check nobody else already uses a name.
fact: The rhythm section is the bass and drums together, which hold the beat; the bass plays the low notes.
fact: Drums are loud enough to annoy neighbours and to damage hearing over time; musicians often wear earplugs made for musicians.
fact: A cover is a song written by someone else; an original is one the band wrote itself.
fact: The Beatles played many cover songs in clubs in Hamburg before they became famous for their own songs.
fact: A rehearsal is a practice session for a performance; a band cannot rehearse full songs if people do not turn up.
fact: Balance means getting each part at the right volume so every part can be heard; being loudest does not make you sound best.
fact: Stage fright, or performance nerves, is fear of performing; many famous performers say they still get it, and it often fades once they start playing.
fact: Feedback is a loud squeal that happens when a microphone picks up sound from a speaker and loops it round and round, louder each time.
fact: Pointing the microphone away from the speakers or turning it down stops feedback.
fact: Many famous bands have split up after arguments about who decides things; good bands talk, vote or take turns.
fact: A setlist is the list of songs a band plans to play.
exercise: Taras's band argues for an hour about the name. What matters more? | Playing music together, because names can change later | Finding the perfect name before doing any practice at all | Choosing a name nobody can pronounce | Printing T-shirts with the name first
exercise: Everyone wants to be the singer and nobody wants bass. Why does that matter? | Without the bass and drums the songs fall apart | It doesn't, the singer is all a band needs | Bass players are only there to carry gear | A band can have five singers and nothing else
exercise: What is the rhythm section? | The bass and drums that hold the beat | The backing singers who do the high parts | The fans who clap along right at the front | The page of music with the rhythms on it
exercise: Josh's brother has a drum kit in the garage. What should the band do? | Agree practice times and protect their ears | Play as loud as they like at any hour | Ignore the neighbours, it's their garage | Skip earplugs, because they enjoy the music and enjoying it keeps ears safe
exercise: Mia says "covers are for losers". What is true? | Most great bands learned by playing other people's songs | Real bands only ever play their own songs, right from the start | Playing covers is against the law | Covers are only for wedding bands
exercise: Where did the Beatles play lots of covers before they were famous? | In clubs in Hamburg | On the Moon | In a stadium in Tokyo | At the Eurovision Song Contest
exercise: Bogdan misses Saturday practice again. What is the fair thing to do? | Turn up when you say, or tell the others early | Skip whenever you like if you are the best player | Send a text after practice has finished | Leave the band without saying anything
exercise: Lachie's amp is so loud the singer cannot hear herself. What is the fix? | Balance the volumes so every part can be heard | Turn every other instrument up even louder | The singer should shout over the guitar | The loudest player always sounds the best
exercise: Rania cannot eat before the school fete. What is she feeling? | Stage fright, which many top performers still get | A sign she should never perform again | Something only beginners ever feel | A sickness from bad food that has nothing to do with being nervous
exercise: A high squeal shoots out of the speakers at the gig. What is it? | Feedback, a sound loop between microphone and speaker | Proof that the speakers are completely broken and must be thrown out | The singer hitting a very high note | Rain getting into the cables
exercise: How do you stop feedback? | Point the microphone away from the speakers or turn it down | Hold the microphone right in front of the speaker so it hears itself | Turn everything up much louder to drown the squeal out | Shout into the microphone louder
exercise: Chloe says, "It's my band, my rules." What usually happens to bands like that? | People feel they have no say and quit | They always become the most famous bands | Everyone is happy because there are no arguments | The band gets better because only one person decides
exercise: Rahul says they can't start until they get proper electric guitars. What is true? | Many musicians started on cheap or home-made instruments | You need expensive gear from a proper shop to make any music at all | Cheap instruments cannot play real songs | Bands are only allowed with a full drum kit
exercise: Carlos has been chatting for half an hour. What helps? | A short plan of which songs to work on | Chatting more until everyone feels ready | Cancelling practice whenever anyone talks | Playing a different song every single minute
exercise: What is a setlist? | The list of songs a band plans to play | The stand that holds the drums | The cable from the guitar to the amp | The name of the band's first album
test: Your mate goes, "real musos never get nervous, mate." Is he right? | No, many top performers say they get nervous before shows | Yes, nerves mean you are not a real musician | Yes, nerves disappear forever after your very first gig, mate | No, only famous people ever get nervous
test: What would happen if the drummer and bass player both quit the night before the gig? | The songs would lose the beat and low notes that hold them up | Nothing, the singer could easily do all of it alone with a microphone | The band would sound better without them | The audience would not notice anything
test: The drummer in Houston yells, "Y'all fixin' to practise or what?" ("fixin' to" means about to). What does he want? | The band to stop chatting and start playing | The band to fix the broken drum kit | The band to go home for the night | The band to practise more chatting
test: In Brisbane the singer calls Lachie a "galah" (a fool, from a noisy parrot). What is she cross about? | His guitar is so loud she can't hear herself | He brought a noisy pet parrot to the band practice | He has not turned up | He is singing her part
test: Chloe says "yeah nah, no arguing." Does she agree with arguing? | No, in Australia "yeah nah" means no | Yes, in Australia "yeah nah" means yes please | She is not sure yet, so she says both words | Yes, she wants a big argument about it all
test: A cartoon shows a band whose speaker squeals because it is "haunted". What is really going on? | The microphone is picking up the speaker in a loop | Ghosts get into speakers at night and make them squeal at the band | The speaker is laughing at the band | Squeals mean the song is a hit
test: Josh says, "Loud music can't hurt your ears if you like it." What is true? | Loud sound damages hearing whether you like it or not | Liking a song makes your ears stronger, so it can't do them any harm | Only music you hate can hurt your ears | Drums are too quiet to hurt anyone
test: Your cousin says, "No band name, no band, end of." What is the best reply? | Play first, the name can come later or change | He's right, the name always has to come first, before anything else | Bands are not allowed to change names | A band with no name cannot play music
test: Rahul's band has one old keyboard, a phone and some buckets. What should they do? | Start playing now with what they have | Wait until they can afford to buy a full studio | Give up because buckets are not instruments | Only practise when they own real guitars
test: Which plan sounds most like a band that will last? | They talk, vote or take turns choosing songs | The founder decides everything without asking | The loudest player in the band always gets their way | Everyone does exactly what they like and never meets
test: Lad in Liverpool says, "Our kid's drum kit is boss, la!" ("our kid" means brother, "boss" means great). What is he saying? | His brother's drum kit is great | His boss owns a drum kit | His child is learning drums | The drum kit belongs to the teacher
exam: Identify the principal cause of the high-pitched sound denominated "feedback" in amplified performance. | The recurrent capture by a microphone of sound emitted by a loudspeaker | The structural failure of the loudspeaker apparatus owing to excessive volume | The production of an exceptionally high note by the vocalist | The ingress of moisture into the electrical cables
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "rhythm section"? | The bass and drums jointly responsible for sustaining the beat | The vocalists entrusted with the uppermost parts | The audience members positioned nearest the stage | The portion of a score in which the rhythms are notated
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the early performances of the Beatles. | The group performed numerous songs by other composers in clubs in Hamburg | The group performed exclusively its own compositions from the outset | The group declined to perform in any club whatsoever | The group commenced its career at the Eurovision Song Contest before performing in any club
exam: Ascertain the course of action most conducive to the continued existence of a band. | The making of decisions jointly, by discussion, vote or rotation | The vesting of all decisions in the founding member alone | The subordination of all members to the loudest performer | The abandonment of any arrangement for joint rehearsal
